1
00:00:00,033 --> 00:00:05,533
Here is a switch. It can be on, or off.

2
00:00:05,533 --> 00:00:14,500
With a few dozen switches, and a list of rules about them, you get one of the most famous puzzles in computer science.

3
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:20,100
For fifty years, every method for solving it has hit the same wall.

4
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:24,767
The work explodes as the puzzle grows.

5
00:00:24,767 --> 00:00:34,067
In October 2026, a paper from OpenAI, written by one of its AI models, claimed a way around that wall.

6
00:00:34,067 --> 00:00:41,800
Not a practical one. But if it is right, it overturns a belief that a whole field was built on.

7
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:47,733
Let's see what that belief was, and how the paper gets around it.

8
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,467
We start with four switches, called A, B, C and D.

9
00:01:00,467 --> 00:01:07,167
And here is a rule. A rule names three switches, and says how it would like each of them.

10
00:01:07,167 --> 00:01:13,767
This one says: A on, or B off, or C on.

11
00:01:13,767 --> 00:01:20,234
The important word is "or". The rule is happy if at least one of its three wishes comes true.

12
00:01:20,234 --> 00:01:25,334
It does not need all three. Just one.

13
00:01:25,334 --> 00:01:34,900
Right now, A is off and C is off, so those two wishes fail. But B is off, and the rule wanted B off.

14
00:01:34,900 --> 00:01:40,000
One wish came true, so the rule is happy.

15
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:47,734
If we flip B on, now none of its wishes come true, and the rule is unhappy.

16
00:01:47,734 --> 00:01:53,300
A real puzzle has many rules. Here are three.

17
00:01:53,300 --> 00:02:01,067
And the puzzle is: can you set the switches so that every rule is happy at the same time?

18
00:02:01,067 --> 00:02:11,100
Let's try. A on makes rule one happy. B on makes rule two happy. C on makes rule three happy. And D can stay off.

19
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:16,034
Every rule is happy. We found a solution.

20
00:02:16,034 --> 00:02:22,867
This puzzle has a name: 3-SAT. Three, because each rule names three switches.

21
00:02:22,867 --> 00:02:30,700
SAT, short for satisfy, because we want to satisfy every rule.

22
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:38,634
It looks like a toy, but a huge range of real problems can be rewritten as this puzzle: making timetables,

23
00:02:38,634 --> 00:02:45,534
checking that a computer chip is designed correctly, planning routes, even solving Sudoku.

24
00:02:45,534 --> 00:02:52,534
That is why computer scientists have studied it more than almost any other problem.

25
00:02:52,534 --> 00:02:58,767
The paper actually answers a harder question. It doesn't just find one good setting.

26
00:02:58,767 --> 00:03:08,234
It counts how many good settings there are. If you can count them, you can certainly tell whether there is at least one.

27
00:03:08,234 --> 00:03:19,000
So: switches, rules, and one question. How many ways are there to make every rule happy?

28
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:31,500
The obvious method is to try every possible setting and check the rules each time. How many settings are there?

29
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:36,133
With one switch, two: off, or on.

30
00:03:36,133 --> 00:03:43,933
Add a second switch, and each of those two settings splits in two. Four settings.

31
00:03:43,933 --> 00:03:49,667
A third switch: eight. A fourth: sixteen.

32
00:03:49,667 --> 00:03:54,633
Every switch you add doubles the number of settings.

33
00:03:54,633 --> 00:04:02,533
Doubling sounds harmless, so let's put numbers on it. Suppose a computer checks a billion settings every second.

34
00:04:02,533 --> 00:04:08,633
With thirty switches it needs about one second. With fifty-five switches, a year.

35
00:04:08,633 --> 00:04:14,600
With eighty-eight switches, longer than the universe has existed.

36
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:20,400
Real puzzles have thousands, or millions, of switches.

37
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:23,033
This kind of growth has a name.

38
00:04:23,033 --> 00:04:32,067
When every extra switch multiplies the work by the same factor, here by two, we call the growth exponential.

39
00:04:32,067 --> 00:04:38,733
Keep an eye on this number: how much the work is multiplied by each extra switch.

40
00:04:38,733 --> 00:04:47,333
It's the measuring stick for the rest of this video. For trying everything, it's times two.

41
00:04:47,333 --> 00:04:55,867
So trying everything is exponential, with a factor of two per switch.

42
00:05:01,100 --> 00:05:09,600
Of course, people have found smarter methods than trying everything. Here is the simplest shortcut.

43
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:11,400
Take any rule.

44
00:05:11,400 --> 00:05:20,800
Either its first wish comes true; or the first fails and the second comes true; or the first two fail and the third comes true.

45
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:25,234
There is no other way to make it happy.

46
00:05:25,234 --> 00:05:29,567
So instead of trying both settings of one switch at a time,

47
00:05:29,567 --> 00:05:37,900
we explore these three cases, and each case settles one, two or three switches at once.

48
00:05:37,900 --> 00:05:44,700
That alone brings the factor per switch down from two to about one point eight four.

49
00:05:44,700 --> 00:05:56,034
Researchers kept improving the tricks. By 1985 the factor was down to one point six. By 1999, one point three three.

50
00:05:56,034 --> 00:06:00,800
Today the best known is about one point three.

51
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:03,300
That is a huge improvement.

52
00:06:03,300 --> 00:06:13,434
For a hundred switches, it is the difference between a one followed by thirty zeros, and about four hundred billion.

53
00:06:13,434 --> 00:06:21,500
But notice what never changed. Each method still multiplies the work by a fixed factor for every switch.

54
00:06:21,500 --> 00:06:29,734
The factor got smaller. It never started to shrink as the puzzle grows.

55
00:06:29,734 --> 00:06:39,034
Every shortcut so far is still exponential. Just a gentler exponential.

56
00:06:44,266 --> 00:06:52,166
So here is the natural question. Can the factor be pushed all the way down toward one?

57
00:06:52,166 --> 00:06:59,400
A factor of one would mean adding a switch no longer multiplies the work at all.

58
00:06:59,400 --> 00:07:09,266
In 2001, two computer scientists, Russell Impagliazzo and Ramamohan Paturi, proposed an answer: no.

59
00:07:09,266 --> 00:07:19,033
There is some floor, a bit above one. Maybe one point zero one, maybe one point two, nobody knows exactly where.

60
00:07:19,033 --> 00:07:24,433
But no method, however clever, will ever get below it.

61
00:07:24,433 --> 00:07:35,433
This is the Exponential Time Hypothesis, or ETH. A hypothesis, because nobody proved it. It's a bet.

62
00:07:35,433 --> 00:07:44,233
But it was a bet that almost everyone took. Researchers built a large body of results on top of it, each of the form:

63
00:07:44,233 --> 00:07:53,533
if ETH is true, then this other problem cannot be solved faster than such and such.

64
00:07:53,533 --> 00:08:04,633
ETH says the wall is real: this puzzle will always be exponential. Many results stand on it.

65
00:08:09,833 --> 00:08:19,633
The new paper claims a method whose factor per switch is not fixed at all. It keeps shrinking as the puzzle grows.

66
00:08:19,633 --> 00:08:28,033
To see what that means, let's compare three kinds of growth, using our measuring stick: the average work per switch.

67
00:08:28,033 --> 00:08:35,900
First, exponential growth. The factor stays the same forever: a flat line.

68
00:08:35,900 --> 00:08:43,966
Now a very different kind of growth: the kind computer scientists consider fast, called polynomial.

69
00:08:43,966 --> 00:08:52,400
Here, doubling the number of switches multiplies the work by a fixed amount, like eight, instead of squaring it.

70
00:08:52,400 --> 00:08:59,866
Spread over all the switches, the work per switch quickly drops to almost one.

71
00:08:59,866 --> 00:09:07,466
And in between, a third kind: the factor per switch keeps shrinking toward one, but slowly.

72
00:09:07,466 --> 00:09:16,700
This is called subexponential. Faster than any exponential, slower than any polynomial.

73
00:09:16,700 --> 00:09:19,833
That's what the paper claims for our puzzle.

74
00:09:19,833 --> 00:09:27,600
A method to count all the good settings, with a cost whose factor per switch sinks toward one.

75
00:09:27,600 --> 00:09:35,066
And here's why that matters. If the factor keeps sinking, it eventually goes below any floor you pick.

76
00:09:35,066 --> 00:09:40,600
So there is no floor. ETH would be false.

77
00:09:40,600 --> 00:09:49,566
For those who like symbols: the cost is roughly two to the power n, divided by the square root of the logarithm of n.

78
00:09:49,566 --> 00:09:58,600
The square root of log n grows forever, very slowly, and that's what makes the factor shrink.

79
00:09:58,600 --> 00:10:09,833
So: a factor that sinks below any floor. That's the claim. Now, how could that possibly work?

80
00:10:15,066 --> 00:10:20,066
The first idea is to turn the puzzle into ordinary arithmetic.

81
00:10:20,066 --> 00:10:34,266
We'll use a smaller puzzle: three switches, and two rules. Rule one: A on, or B off, or C on. Rule two: B on, or C on.

82
00:10:34,266 --> 00:10:41,333
For any setting, give each rule a score: one if the rule is happy, zero if it is not.

83
00:10:41,333 --> 00:10:48,533
With every switch off, rule one is happy, because it wants B off, so it scores one.

84
00:10:48,533 --> 00:10:54,433
Rule two wants B or C on, so it scores zero.

85
00:10:54,433 --> 00:11:00,300
Now multiply the scores. One times zero is zero.

86
00:11:00,300 --> 00:11:08,000
When is the product one? Only when every rule scores one, that is, when every rule is happy.

87
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:13,133
A single unhappy rule makes the whole product zero.

88
00:11:13,133 --> 00:11:19,300
So go through all eight settings, write down each product, and add them up.

89
00:11:19,300 --> 00:11:25,700
The total, five, is exactly the number of good settings.

90
00:11:25,700 --> 00:11:34,400
We've turned counting into a big sum of products. That alone doesn't save any work: we still visited every setting.

91
00:11:34,400 --> 00:11:40,266
But sums and products are things algebra knows how to rearrange.

92
00:11:41,300 --> 00:11:42,934
Here's the dream.

93
00:11:42,934 --> 00:11:54,067
Suppose the switches split into two teams, and no rule mixes the teams: every rule only talks about switches in one team.

94
00:11:54,067 --> 00:12:01,834
Then the teams can't affect each other. Count the good settings of team one on its own. Say there are three hundred.

95
00:12:01,834 --> 00:12:06,900
Count team two on its own: say five hundred.

96
00:12:06,900 --> 00:12:12,534
Every good setting of team one can be combined with every good setting of team two.

97
00:12:12,534 --> 00:12:17,567
So the total is three hundred times five hundred.

98
00:12:17,567 --> 00:12:24,234
And the work collapses. Trying all forty switches at once means about a trillion settings.

99
00:12:24,234 --> 00:12:30,867
Two teams of twenty, counted separately, need about two million.

100
00:12:30,867 --> 00:12:39,600
Of course, real puzzles aren't like this. However you split the switches, some rules reach across.

101
00:12:40,633 --> 00:12:44,100
But there's a middle ground. Look at this rule.

102
00:12:44,100 --> 00:12:53,033
It crosses between teams: it mentions A from team one, and X and Y from team two.

103
00:12:53,033 --> 00:13:01,467
Notice what the rule needs to know about team two. Not which of X and Y is on: only how many of them are on.

104
00:13:01,467 --> 00:13:07,333
Zero, one, or two. Let's call that number a total.

105
00:13:07,333 --> 00:13:16,200
Now fix the total, say at zero. Then the crossing rule only cares about team one, and the teams separate again.

106
00:13:16,200 --> 00:13:24,967
Count the good settings of team one, count the good settings of team two that have exactly that total, and multiply.

107
00:13:24,967 --> 00:13:32,633
Then do the same with the total fixed at one, and at two. Add the three answers up.

108
00:13:32,633 --> 00:13:38,900
So teams that share only a few totals can still be counted almost separately.

109
00:13:38,900 --> 00:13:49,500
The price: one round for every possible value of the totals. That's affordable, as long as there are only a few totals.

110
00:13:50,533 --> 00:13:58,333
In real puzzles, rules need to know much more than a few totals. So the paper does something surprising.

111
00:13:58,333 --> 00:14:04,100
It doesn't change the puzzle. It changes the arithmetic.

112
00:14:04,100 --> 00:14:09,733
Remember the sum of products from step one. The paper adds a dial to it.

113
00:14:09,733 --> 00:14:19,033
Inside every rule, each switch is replaced by a blend of switches, and the dial controls the blend.

114
00:14:19,033 --> 00:14:25,867
At zero, there is no blending at all: the sum is exactly our count.

115
00:14:25,867 --> 00:14:32,467
And at some special dial settings, one, two, three, and so on,

116
00:14:32,467 --> 00:14:41,267
the blends are arranged so that every rule sees switches from its own team, plus only a few totals from the other teams.

117
00:14:41,267 --> 00:14:45,600
Exactly the situation of step three.

118
00:14:45,600 --> 00:14:49,900
So at those settings, the sum is fast to compute.

119
00:14:49,900 --> 00:14:58,633
But there's a catch: with the blending switched on, the result isn't counting anything any more. It's just a number.

120
00:14:58,633 --> 00:15:04,567
The count we want sits at zero, where the shortcut doesn't work.

121
00:15:04,567 --> 00:15:12,967
We can compute quickly at one, two, three. We need the answer at zero. How do we get there?

122
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:20,700
Picture turning the dial slowly and watching the number change. It traces a curve.

123
00:15:20,700 --> 00:15:31,167
The paper shows that this curve is of a simple kind, a polynomial, which means it can only wiggle a limited number of times.

124
00:15:31,167 --> 00:15:39,400
We know three points on the curve: at one, two and three. We want its height at zero.

125
00:15:39,400 --> 00:15:42,234
But three points are not enough.

126
00:15:42,234 --> 00:15:49,834
Here are several curves, all allowed the same number of wiggles, all passing through the same three points.

127
00:15:49,834 --> 00:15:53,500
At zero, they disagree.

128
00:15:53,500 --> 00:15:57,600
So the method collects more information at each point.

129
00:15:57,600 --> 00:16:07,767
Not just the height, but the slope: how steeply the curve is climbing there, like the steepness of a hill under your feet.

130
00:16:07,767 --> 00:16:18,667
Once the slopes at one, two and three are known too, only one curve fits. And at zero, it lands on fourteen.

131
00:16:18,667 --> 00:16:29,800
This is a real example, computed by our own code, with six switches. Trying every setting confirms the answer: fourteen.

132
00:16:29,800 --> 00:16:33,100
For a bigger puzzle the curve can wiggle more,

133
00:16:33,100 --> 00:16:40,167
so you need more information at each point: the slope, how the curve bends, and so on.

134
00:16:40,167 --> 00:16:50,267
But the principle is the same: enough information at the easy points pins down the whole curve, and with it, the count.

135
00:16:51,300 --> 00:16:58,634
Here is the hard part, and the heart of the paper. Every extra piece of information costs work.

136
00:16:58,634 --> 00:17:06,267
So the method only pays off if the curve wiggles little, while there are many easy points to measure it at.

137
00:17:06,267 --> 00:17:10,834
Those two goals pull against each other.

138
00:17:10,834 --> 00:17:16,500
The paper's main invention is a construction that achieves both at once:

139
00:17:16,500 --> 00:17:21,634
a clever way of blending in which most of the mixing cancels itself out,

140
00:17:21,634 --> 00:17:27,434
and only a small leftover needs to be tracked with totals.

141
00:17:27,434 --> 00:17:34,000
Finally, all of this works best when each switch appears in only a few rules.

142
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:42,534
The paper shows that any puzzle can be broken into tidy pieces like that, without changing the count.

143
00:17:42,534 --> 00:17:48,167
Let's put it together. Turn counting into arithmetic. Add a dial.

144
00:17:48,167 --> 00:17:55,767
At special settings, the puzzle splits into teams that share only a few totals, so it's fast.

145
00:17:55,767 --> 00:18:03,100
Measure the curve there, and follow it back to zero, where the true count lives.

146
00:18:03,100 --> 00:18:09,800
Adding up all the costs, each part grows more slowly than any fixed factor per switch.

147
00:18:09,800 --> 00:18:14,634
And that is the claim: subexponential.

148
00:18:17,866 --> 00:18:25,466
So, is this a fast new way to solve puzzles? No. And it isn't close.

149
00:18:25,466 --> 00:18:29,566
The method only wins as puzzles grow without limit.

150
00:18:29,566 --> 00:18:37,500
We took the explicit numbers in the paper's overview and worked out when it starts to pay off.

151
00:18:37,500 --> 00:18:44,066
It beats simply trying everything when the number of switches has about eighteen billion digits.

152
00:18:44,066 --> 00:18:51,766
Not eighteen billion switches. A number of switches eighteen billion digits long.

153
00:18:51,766 --> 00:18:58,100
To beat the best older method, about three hundred and forty billion digits.

154
00:18:58,100 --> 00:19:06,166
For comparison, the number of atoms in the observable universe has about eighty digits.

155
00:19:06,166 --> 00:19:14,533
These are rough figures, from an analysis that wasn't trying to be efficient, so the true crossover could be much earlier.

156
00:19:14,533 --> 00:19:21,533
But nobody expects this method to run on a real computer.

157
00:19:21,533 --> 00:19:29,666
Its importance is not what it does. It's what it proves is possible.

158
00:19:33,900 --> 00:19:40,434
If the proof is right, ETH is false, and so are its stronger cousins.

159
00:19:40,434 --> 00:19:45,467
Results that started with "if ETH is true" lose their footing.

160
00:19:45,467 --> 00:19:53,134
Some can be rescued from other assumptions that are still believed. Others will need rethinking.

161
00:19:53,134 --> 00:19:58,934
What about the most famous question in computer science, P versus NP?

162
00:19:58,934 --> 00:20:05,834
It's one of the seven Millennium Prize Problems, with a million-dollar prize.

163
00:20:05,834 --> 00:20:14,667
Roughly, it asks whether puzzles like ours can be solved with polynomial growth, the fast kind.

164
00:20:14,667 --> 00:20:24,100
The new method is subexponential, which is still slower than polynomial. So P versus NP stays open.

165
00:20:24,100 --> 00:20:33,800
It doesn't break encryption: everyday encryption doesn't depend on ETH, and the method is far too slow anyway.

166
00:20:33,800 --> 00:20:37,034
And it doesn't give faster puzzle solvers:

167
00:20:37,034 --> 00:20:45,934
practical solvers already handle huge real-world puzzles with tricks that work well in practice.

168
00:20:45,934 --> 00:20:54,000
What it changes is our understanding of what is possible in principle.

169
00:20:58,233 --> 00:21:02,100
Is it true? It's too early to say for sure.

170
00:21:02,100 --> 00:21:10,433
The paper is a preprint, not yet checked by peer review, and it was written by an AI model.

171
00:21:10,433 --> 00:21:20,133
OpenAI reports that part of it has been checked by computer, in a system called Lean, where a machine verifies every logical step.

172
00:21:20,133 --> 00:21:28,067
That check covers a slightly weaker version of the result, which would still break ETH.

173
00:21:28,067 --> 00:21:40,067
And a group of experts, including Impagliazzo and Paturi, who proposed ETH in the first place, is examining the proof.

174
00:21:40,067 --> 00:21:47,933
We also ran the method's main steps on small puzzles with our own code, and it produces the exact counts.

175
00:21:47,933 --> 00:21:55,467
That can't confirm the proof for large puzzles, but it shows the ideas fit together.

176
00:21:55,500 --> 00:22:03,367
Let's recap. A puzzle of switches and rules, where trying everything doubles the work with every switch.

177
00:22:03,367 --> 00:22:15,167
Fifty years of shortcuts that made the growth gentler, but never changed its kind. A bet, ETH, that it never could.

178
00:22:15,167 --> 00:22:21,034
And now a paper that blends the puzzle with a dial, solves it at easy points,

179
00:22:21,034 --> 00:22:28,167
and follows a curve back to the true count, with a cost whose factor keeps sinking.

180
00:22:28,167 --> 00:22:33,900
If it holds up, the wall is still there for every puzzle we could ever build.

181
00:22:33,900 --> 00:22:40,734
But in principle, it was never a wall at all.
